.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-wallets-tokens-add - Accept a token on a wallet (re-enables a disabled holding)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 wallets tokens add  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Accept a token, on one chain, on a wallet (by id or 0x address).

.PP
An upsert: a holding that was disabled is re-enabled rather than duplicated.
--token is a symbol (USDC) resolved on --chain-id, or the token's 0x address.
--default makes it the wallet's default method, demoting the previous one;
--chain-id falls back to the configured default ('rail0 config set chain-id').


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--chain-id\fP=0
	Chain of the token (default: the configured chain-id)

.PP
\fB--default\fP[=false]
	Make it the wallet's default payment method

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for add

.PP
\fB-t\fP, \fB--token\fP=""
	Token symbol (e.g. USDC) or 0x address (default: the configured token)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 wallets tokens add 0xABCD… --chain-id 84532 --token USDC
  rail0 wallets tokens add 0xABCD… --chain-id 84532 --token 0x036C… --default
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-wallets-tokens(1)\fP
